QCA Windows GSS plugin version 2.0.0
------------------------------------
Date: May 29th, 2008
Website: http://delta.affinix.com/qca/
Mailing List: Delta Project <delta@lists.affinix.com>

Author: Justin Karneges <justin@affinix.com>

This plugin provides SASL (GSSAPI mechanism only) using the native Windows
Security Support Provider Interface (SSPI) for Kerberos.  Basically what this
means is that if your Windows computer authenticates against a domain, then
you can access capable SASL servers on that domain without a password.

The provider is built on Windows with the Qt 5 or Qt 6 CMake build. It uses
the system SSPI library and does not require OpenSSL or a separate GSSAPI
library. A configured Kerberos environment and suitable logged-in credentials
are required for an actual GSSAPI exchange.

Capabilities and limits
-----------------------

Native SSPI implements client-side GSSAPI using the logged-in user's credentials.
It supports authentication with no SASL security layer, integrity protection,
or confidentiality, subject to the caller's constraints and the server's offer.
Confidentiality is conservatively reported as SSF 56; larger requested minimum
values are rejected. Incoming protected records are limited to 8192 bytes.
The record decoder retains at most one partial record and processes streams
without repeatedly copying the unconsumed remainder.

Other client mechanisms and server operations are delegated to another installed
SASL provider. SCRAM and SCRAM-PLUS therefore require such a provider; SSPI itself
does not implement them. Channel binding is forwarded to the selected delegate.
Native GSSAPI does not implement channel binding, so a critical binding request
cannot select that native path or silently discard the binding.

Automated tests cover provider loading, delegation, complete SCRAM-PLUS exchanges
when a compatible delegate is available, SSPI buffer ownership and handle cleanup,
fragmented records, size limits and large streams. SSPI session tests use a fake
function table and require no domain credentials. A separate test loads the
actual system library and enumerates its security packages. These checks do
not replace testing Kerberos interoperability against a configured domain.

Installing
----------

For Windows:

  cmake -S . -B build -DBUILD_WITH_QT6=ON -DBUILD_PLUGINS=wingss
  cmake --build build --config Release
  cmake --install build --config Release

Use BUILD_WITH_QT6=OFF for Qt 5. The Windows "auto" and "all" plugin selections
also include qca-wingss. Shared and static provider builds are supported.
